WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.870 --> 00:00:06.170 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Hi, I'm Shi Feng, a PhD student at Cornell University.

2
00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:10.450 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In this video, we will talk about the optimal matching problem.

3
00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:18.558 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In particular, I want to share the main result and ideas from these two papers,

4
00:00:18.558 --> 00:00:24.390 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
both studying how the optimal matching cost fluctuates when the points are random.

5
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:28.370 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
They address different dimensions using very different methods.

6
00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:34.430 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The first, written with Gilles Mordant, treats dimensions two, three and 4.

7
00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.710 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The second treats dimensions 5 and higher.

8
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.920 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Here is the setup of the optimal matching problem.

9
00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.880 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Imagine a company with several factories.

10
00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:49.720 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Their locations are chosen randomly in this square.

11
00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.540 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Workers live throughout the square, spread evenly across it.

12
00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.311 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We treat them as a continuous population. The question is simple.

13
00:01:00.311 --> 00:01:03.950 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
How should we assign workers to factories?

14
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:07.920 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
To see what an assignment means, look at this worker's commute.

15
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.820 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
An assignment gives a destination to every worker.

16
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:17.860 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This divides the square into regions, one for each factory. We call this a partition.

17
00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.100 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The colored regions collect the workers who go to the same factory.

18
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.580 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Everyone in the green regions goes to the green factory.

19
00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:32.660 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
But not every assignment is allowed. There is a staffing requirement.

20
00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:36.602 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Every factory needs the same share of workers.

21
00:01:36.602 --> 00:01:41.478 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
With six factories, each get one sixth of the population.

22
00:01:41.478 --> 00:01:47.116 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Because the workers are spread evenly, their regions must have equal area.

23
00:01:47.116 --> 00:01:56.336 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For example, if every worker goes to the nearest factory, some factories may receive more workers and others fewer.

24
00:01:56.336 --> 00:01:59.360 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This would not meet our equal staffing requirement.

25
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.160 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Now let's define the transportation cost we want to optimize.

26
00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:15.720 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
If a worker lives at x and travel to the factory at xi, one natural cost is the distance dx xi.

27
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:21.600 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This is a well-established model but the corresponding fluctuation problem is still open.

28
00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:29.940 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In this project, we instead use the squared distance dx xi squared. This is also a reasonable model.

29
00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:35.680 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
A ten-hour flight can feel more than twice as exhausting as a five-hour flight.

30
00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:40.720 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
With this cost in place, we can now choose the best assignment.

31
00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:49.200 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
After fixing the randomly chosen factory locations, we can partition the square into regions, one for each factory.

32
00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:54.920 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We want the smallest total transportation cost while keeping the worker share equal.

33
00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:59.400 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Finding the best assignment is the optimal matching problem.

34
00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.760 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
With squared distance cost, an optimal assignment exists.

35
00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.220 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
It divides the square into convex polygons.

36
00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.620 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This simple geometry is one advantage of squared distance cost.

37
00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.140 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The same problem can be posed in higher dimensions.

38
00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:21.580 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimension 3, the unit square becomes a unit cube.

39
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.660 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Here, 3 factories sits on its front face.

40
00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:32.640 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Each colored region contains the worker assigned to one factory and extends through the cube.

41
00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.080 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Each occupies one third of its volume.

42
00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:42.621 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
These regions are convex polytopes, the 3-dimensional counterparts of convex polygons.

43
00:03:42.621 --> 00:03:48.840 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Higher dimensional geometry is difficult to visualize, especially beyond dimension 3.

44
00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:54.520 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
So we will use 2-dimensional drawings with convex polygons to illustrate the ideas.

45
00:03:55.940 --> 00:04:01.180 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For the rest of the story, imagine that opposite edges of the square are joined.

46
00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:05.200 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Leave through the left edge and you return through the right.

47
00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:08.680 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This gives us a world without a boundary.

48
00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:15.620 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The two-dimensional torus, called T2, with side length one in each direction.

49
00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:20.860 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This Distances use the shortest route, including routes that wrap around.

50
00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:31.120 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimension three, we join each face of the unit cube to its opposite side, leaving through one face brings you back through the opposite side.

51
00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:34.400 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This is the 3-dimensional torus T3.

52
00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:43.660 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In D dimensions, we make the same identification on the unit cube to obtain Td, with side length 1 in each direction.

53
00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:52.200 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We work on the torus because it removes boundary effects and gives us access to additional mathematical tools.

54
00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:55.920 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We won't discuss these advantages in details here.

55
00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.860 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The paper explains how they enter the proofs.

56
00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:05.780 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We now have a concrete model and a question. Let's define everything clearly.

57
00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:12.540 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Write Wn for the optimal matching cost of n factories at X1 to Xn in TD.

58
00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:20.720 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For each factory xi, let ai be its assigned worker region. This region partitioned the torus.

59
00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.780 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The uniform worker measure mu has total mass 1.

60
00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:29.380 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
So equal staffing means mu of Ai equals one over n.

61
00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:38.480 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
With dx xi denoting the torus distance, the integral adds the squared distance transportation cost across ai.

62
00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:45.680 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We sum over all factories, then minimize over all partitions satisfying this equal staffing requirement.

63
00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:53.820 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Because the factory locations are random, the matching cost WN is also random.

64
00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:57.800 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Now let the number of factories n become large.

65
00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:02.280 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
How does the average transport cost depend on n?

66
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.060 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
How large is the variation measured by the variance?

67
00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:13.340 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
And after subtracting the average and rescaling the fluctuations, do we see a fixed limiting distribution?

68
00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:17.140 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
These are the three questions we want to answer.

69
00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:22.180 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Let's first try to understand the expectation of the matching cost.

70
00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:29.620 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
More factories mean shorter transportation distances, so how quickly does the expectation decrease as n grows?

71
00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:37.180 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Suppose we temporarily ignore the staffing requirement that every factory needs to receive an equal share of workers.

72
00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.019 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Each worker can then go to the nearest factory.

73
00:06:41.019 --> 00:06:48.448 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
On the d-dimensional torus, The typical distance to that factory is of order n to the power of negative 1 over d.

74
00:06:48.448 --> 00:06:57.940 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Since our cost is squared distance, this gives a first guess that expectation of wn is of order n to the power of negative 2 over d.

75
00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:02.200 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This nearest-factory guess is incorrect in dimension two.

76
00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.380 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The blue arrow points to the nearest factory.

77
00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:09.914 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The orange arrow points to the factory worker is actually assigned to,

78
00:07:09.914 --> 00:07:14.500 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
which may be farther away because every factory must receive an equal share of workers.

79
00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:22.316 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimension 2, a typical worker needs to travel a distance of order square root of log n over square root of n,

80
00:07:22.316 --> 00:07:25.120 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
instead of one over the square root of n.

81
00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:31.840 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For dimensions three and larger, both distance scales are n to the power of negative 1 over d.

82
00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:35.480 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
So the nearest-factory guess has the correct order.

83
00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:40.960 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Beyond the unexpected log n factor, there is another surprise.

84
00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:45.800 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimension 2, the leading constant is exactly 1 over 4 pi.

85
00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:51.120 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Now that we understand the expectation, let's look at the fluctuations.

86
00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:55.860 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Each picture has six factories, but their locations differ.

87
00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:59.360 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The workers are spread uniformly in all three pictures.

88
00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:06.260 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
On the left, the factories are clustered, so workers must travel farther and the matching cost is high.

89
00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:11.600 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In the middle, the factories are placed randomly, giving an intermediate cost.

90
00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:16.400 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
On the right, they are more evenly spread, giving a lower cost.

91
00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:22.440 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The numbers show the computed optimal cost for this particular pictures.

92
00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:27.560 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
When the factory locations are random, how large is the variance of Wn?

93
00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:33.460 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
After centering and rescaling, do its fluctuation approach a limiting distribution?

94
00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:41.820 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Our two papers answers these two questions and the reveal unexpected behavior in both the variance and the limiting distribution.

95
00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:45.940 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimensions two and three, the variance has the same scale.

96
00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:49.680 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimension four, an extra log factor appears.

97
00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:55.000 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimensions 5 and higher, the variance grows in a more regular way.

98
00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:58.940 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The limiting distribution also has an interesting transition.

99
00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:05.080 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
It is non-Gaussian in dimensions two and three, but Gaussian from dimension 4 and onward.

100
00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:12.800 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In these unusual low-dimensional cases, we can also identify the exact leading constant in the variance.

101
00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:18.260 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimension 2 and 3, the constants are given by explicit lattice sum.

102
00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:22.700 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimension 4, the constant has a simple closed form.

103
00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:29.960 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Interestingly, all three constants also involve pi, just like the expectation constant we saw earlier.

104
00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:33.980 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
These results show two different critical dimensions.

105
00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:39.240 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Dimension 2 is special for expectation because of the extra log n factor.

106
00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:41.960 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Dimension 4 is special for fluctuations.

107
00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:46.420 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Both the variance scale and the limiting distribution change there.

108
00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:49.400 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
These results are both surprising and precise.

109
00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:54.300 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
How can we obtain such a detailed understanding of these unusual fluctuations?

110
00:09:55.820 --> 00:10:03.160 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Explicit limiting distributions are difficult to obtain in random optimization problems, especially non-Gaussian limits.

111
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:10.280 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Classical examples where such limiting distributions are known include the exactly solvable models in the KPZ family.

112
00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:17.400 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We won't explain these models here, but such precise results are possible because the exact formula are available.

113
00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:24.938 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For example, the formula shown here expresses the cumulative distribution function as a Fredholm determinant.

114
00:10:24.938 --> 00:10:27.420 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For optimal matching, the reason is different.

115
00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:32.840 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Kantorovich duality is the main tool, and it's the starting point of the whole argument.

116
00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:37.640 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
There are many ways to understand Kantorovich duality.

117
00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:45.480 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Here, I will use an interpretation that I find intuitive for this problem, although it's not the usual textbook presentation.

118
00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:48.340 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Recall our original problem.

119
00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:56.720 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The factories are placed randomly, and we divide the workers into regions like this, assigning each region to one factory.

120
00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:04.900 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Each factory must receive equal share of worker, and we want to minimize the total squared distance transportation cost.

121
00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:11.700 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Now, instead of assigning each worker to a factory, we let the workers choose for themselves.

122
00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:16.240 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
What we do is assign different wages to different factories.

123
00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:25.840 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
If factory xi offers wage wi, a worker at x receives a net wage of wi minus dx xi square.

124
00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:29.740 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The wage minus the squared distance transportation cost.

125
00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:33.400 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The worker chooses the factory where this is the largest.

126
00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:37.840 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
A higher wage can therefore make a farther factory more attractive.

127
00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:41.780 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Arbitrary wages usually do not give equal staffing.

128
00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:46.460 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
One factory may attract too many workers. Another too few.

129
00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:50.540 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Changing wages moves the boundary of the worker regions.

130
00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:58.800 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Keeping the other wages fixed, increasing a factory's wage attracts more workers and expands its region.

131
00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:01.860 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Decreasing its wage has the opposite effect.

132
00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:06.260 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Fewer workers choose that factory and its region shrinks.

133
00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:12.060 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Wage changes let us rebalance staffing without assigning each worker individually.

134
00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:21.023 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Kantorovich duality tells us that we can choose wages so that every factory receives the equal share of the workers,

135
00:12:21.023 --> 00:12:24.560 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
and their choice minimizes the total transportation cost.

136
00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:28.880 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This turns the matching problem into a search for balancing wages.

137
00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:36.062 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Economically, we can think of this as market adjusting towards the optimal assignment.

138
00:12:36.062 --> 00:12:45.941 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Suppose every factory starts with an arbitrary wage, some attract more than their required share of workers, while others attract fewer.

139
00:12:45.941 --> 00:12:51.740 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
An overstaffed factory lowers its wage; an understaffed factory raise its wage.

140
00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:58.240 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
With an appropriate gradual adjustment rule, this process converges to balanced staffing.

141
00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:02.820 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
At that point, the worker choice gives an optimal assignment.

142
00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:08.778 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Mathematically, Kantorovich duality says there is a wage vector w star,

143
00:13:08.778 --> 00:13:15.157 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
whose induced partition satisfies the staffing requirement, and for which this equality holds.

144
00:13:15.157 --> 00:13:21.570 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The total worker population has mass one, and each factory hires a share one over n.

145
00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:25.420 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
So the first term is the total wage paid by the company.

146
00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:30.280 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For a worker at X, the maximum is the best net wage available.

147
00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:39.500 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The factory wage minus the squared distance transportation cost, integrating adds these best net wage over all workers.

148
00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:48.240 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The difference between the company's total wage and the workers' total net wage is the transportation cost of this optimal partition.

149
00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:53.840 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We now return to the fluctuation question in dimension 2, 3 and 4.

150
00:13:54.380 --> 00:14:00.974 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Starting from Kantorovich duality, we use what is called the linearization optimal transport.

151
00:14:00.974 --> 00:14:02.840 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We won't explain this step here.

152
00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:13.380 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This gives an approximation that decompose the matching cost into a mean zero random part shown in blue and a deterministic part shown in orange.

153
00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:18.280 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Here, K is a cutoff controlling how much detail we retain.

154
00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:26.458 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Both the blue and orange term have simple but non-obvious formula, which are given in our low-dimensional paper.

155
00:14:26.458 --> 00:14:31.620 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The blue part controls the fluctuation, while the orange part controls the expectation.

156
00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:35.600 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
First, consider the expectation in dimension 2.

157
00:14:36.100 --> 00:14:40.940 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For the leading expectation, we take the cutoff k equals to square root of n.

158
00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:46.400 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This cutoff is motivated by natural scale of smoothing, which we won't discuss here.

159
00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:54.340 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Since the blue term has mean 0, the leading-order term of the expectation is approximated by the orange term.

160
00:14:54.340 --> 00:14:59.260 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This is indeed correct, and gives the leading constant after proper scaling.

161
00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:03.540 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Understanding the fluctuation is the main object of our paper.

162
00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:12.020 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We proved that the blue term captures the fluctuation of the matching cost accurately enough to recover both the variance and the limiting distribution.

163
00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:17.420 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The variance of WN is therefore asymptotic to that of the blue term.

164
00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:23.314 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
After centering and dividing by their standard deviation, they have the same limiting law.

165
00:15:23.314 --> 00:15:27.540 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Non-Gaussian in dimensions two and three, and Gaussian in dimension four.

166
00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:34.380 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Why doesn't this approximation work in dimensions five and higher? let's look at those two graphs.

167
00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:41.380 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Each point along the horizontal axis represents a different factory configuration ordered by matching cost.

168
00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:47.640 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The dark curve shows the matching cost fluctuation, and the blue curve shows the simpler approximation.

169
00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:51.480 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Their values spread out along the vertical axis.

170
00:15:52.520 --> 00:15:55.578 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The shape beside it represents their distribution.

171
00:15:55.578 --> 00:16:02.120 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimensions 2, 3, and 4, the curves are close enough to recover both the variance and limiting distribution.

172
00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:07.140 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The separation on the right illustrated the problem in higher dimensions.

173
00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:12.080 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The approximation error is no longer controlled at the fluctuation scale,

174
00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:17.080 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
So we cannot conclude that the two have the same variance and the limiting distribution.

175
00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:22.100 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We therefore need a different method, which is the subject of the second paper.

176
00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:26.900 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For dimensions 5 and higher, we turn to a perturbation method.

177
00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:30.880 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
On the left, we remove the circled green factory.

178
00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:37.660 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
On the right, we build the new purple factory at an independent, uniformly random location.

179
00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:41.200 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
All other factory locations stay fixed.

180
00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:48.080 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Both pictures shows optimal assignments with equal worker shares, although the region boundary changes.

181
00:16:48.680 --> 00:16:53.040 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We ask how much this replacement changes the optimal matching cost.

182
00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:55.980 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
once we understand this change precisely enough.

183
00:16:56.560 --> 00:17:02.760 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We can understand the fluctuation using standard method in probability, which we won't explain in detail here.

184
00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:11.340 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
To study this replacement, replacement, we gradually shifted the required worker share from the old green factory to the new purple one.

185
00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:18.571 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The colored region shows staffing, and the bar shows their shares change as T move from 0 to 1.

186
00:17:18.571 --> 00:17:25.260 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Write WN0 for the initial optimal cost, and the WN1 for the cost after replacement.

187
00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:35.460 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
At time T, the share at the old factory decreased to 1 minus T over N, while the share in new factory increase to T over N.

188
00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:42.100 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Every other factory keeps its location and shares one over N. But the region boundary adjusts.

189
00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:46.920 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We will optimize at every stage and call the cost W N T.

190
00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:52.540 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We want to study the change in optimal cost, W N 1 minus W N 0.

191
00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:58.100 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Integrating the derivative along this interpolation gives the identity on the screen.

192
00:17:58.580 --> 00:18:04.680 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We therefore need to understand the derivative of Wn(t). How does the optimal cost respond?

193
00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:11.920 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
When we increase the new factories, require the worker share and decrease the old factories by the same amount.

194
00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:18.999 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
To study this cost derivative, we return to wages using Kantorovich duality.

195
00:18:18.999 --> 00:18:23.740 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Let w be the wage vector and let m be the staffing vector.

196
00:18:24.500 --> 00:18:31.740 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In both vector, the new factory comes first, the old factory second, followed by the other n minus one factories.

197
00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:37.500 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The staffing share changes as shown, and the optimal wages adjust.

198
00:18:38.060 --> 00:18:42.480 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Kantorovich duality connects these two vectors to the transportation cost.

199
00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:48.920 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
It let us calculate how change in staffing affect the wages and the optimal cost.

200
00:18:49.540 --> 00:18:56.100 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Recall that we want to understand how the optimal cost WN changes as the staffing vector m changes.

201
00:18:56.840 --> 00:19:01.900 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Kantorovich duality lets us study this through the optimal wage vector w?

202
00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:06.700 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Staffing changes the wage, which determines the change in cost.

203
00:19:07.240 --> 00:19:17.480 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Since the staffing changes along the interpolation are known, the derivative matrix we need to understand is h inverse, which is dw over dm.

204
00:19:17.480 --> 00:19:24.265 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We have a more direct understanding of what H equals to Dm over Dw is.

205
00:19:24.265 --> 00:19:32.200 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The staffing response to wages. The next slide shows why. Why is H easier to understand?

206
00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:35.620 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Change one wage and watch how staffing responds.

207
00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:40.560 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Keep every other wage fixed and increase the green factory's wage.

208
00:19:41.620 --> 00:19:45.540 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Workers near its boundary now prefer it, so the region expands.

209
00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:52.300 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Since workers are uniformly distributed, the region's area is exactly the worker's share.

210
00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:58.180 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Its own staffing increases, while factories sharing a boundary lose workers.

211
00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:06.960 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
These responses form the corresponding column of H: a positive entry on the green factory and negative entries for its neighbors.

212
00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:12.640 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This slide is for viewers familiar with matrices and Markov processes.

213
00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:17.960 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We want to understand H inverse, but have a direct information about H.

214
00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:21.220 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This integral representation connects the two.

215
00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:25.680 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We now think of negative H as the generator of a random walk.

216
00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:35.920 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For a fixed partition, the matrix e to the power of negative SH describes a continuous-time random walk between neighboring factory region.

217
00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:40.960 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The region matters because the jump rate depends on the shared boundary size.

218
00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:46.500 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We can therefore study the inverse through the behavior of this walk.

219
00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:55.260 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
To sum up, we have connected the effect of replacing one factory to the behavior of random walk on the factory regions.

220
00:20:55.840 --> 00:21:01.720 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The connection passes through how the optimal wages respond when we perturb the staffing requirement.

221
00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:08.709 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This lets us bring the tools from Markov-process theory to control the change in optimal optimal cost

222
00:21:08.709 --> 00:21:13.000 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
and prove the fluctuation result for Wn in dimensions 5 and higher.

223
00:21:14.740 --> 00:21:18.680 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This video is meant to convey intuition, not rigorous proofs.

224
00:21:19.420 --> 00:21:26.100 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The explanation are deliberately informal, and some statements are even incorrect without additional explanation.

225
00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:34.580 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For example, H is not invertible on the full space, so our notation H inverse needs a precise interpretation.

226
00:21:34.580 --> 00:21:41.320 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For a rigorous account of these ideas, I encourage you to read the proof strategy section in the two papers.

227
00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:45.500 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
If you would like to go deeper, the full proofs are in the papers.
